  <static seed="54" source="Contrib/CUNY/CityTech/CollegeAlgebra_Trig/setLinesReview/from-graph-ratl-inter.pg">
    <statement><p>Find an equation for the line graphed below:</p>
<!-- PTX:WARNING: PGML wanted to center align here. -->
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/linear-eqn-from-graph-1-image-1.png" width="66%"/>
<p><fillin characters="32" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><ul label="disc">
<li>
<p>Do not use decimal approximations in your answer.</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</statement>
    <hint><p>Start by identifying a couple of <lq/>nice<rq/> points on the grid that the line passes through.</p>
<p>Once have two points for your line to pass through, you can determine the slope.</p>
<p>Use the point-slope form of a line. <m>y = m (x-x_A) + y_A</m></p>
</hint>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>y = \frac{4}{3}\mathopen{}\left(x+1\right)-2</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>The graphed line passes through the points: <m>A = (-4,-6)</m>, <m>B = (-1,-2)</m>, <m>C = (2,2)</m></p>
<p><alert>Select any pair of points on the line to compute the slope:</alert></p>
<p><m>m = \frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x} = \frac{-2 + 6}{-1 + 4} = {{\frac{4}{3}}}</m></p>
<p><alert>Use the point-slope form of a line:</alert></p>
<p><m>y = m (x - x_A) + y_A</m></p>
<p><m>y = {{\frac{4}{3}}\mathopen{}\left(x+1\right)-2}</m></p>
</solution>
